and p.11, "Administrative Distance":
  > "The [110/2] beside the route in an IP route table means the Administrative Distance (AD) is
  >  110 (OSPF has an AD of 110) and the Metric of the route is 2."

**The objective this case is graded against**, since none is authored: OSPFv2 on the DUT must
(1) be absent by default and gated behind `service ospf`; (2) form an adjacency with a real
neighbour; (3) install a route LEARNED FROM THE PROTOCOL (code `O`, from an identified
neighbour) for a prefix reachable no other way, and FORWARD frames over it, while a control
flow to an unrouted prefix is dropped in the same leg; (4) stop forwarding when the
advertisement is WITHDRAWN, proven on the wire and not merely in the table; (5) be removable
from the dataplane; and (6) survive a reboot.  Depth beyond the floor per the 2026-10-08
"satisfactory level of testing" rule: two neighbours over two different interfaces so a
single-prefix withdrawal has a live positive control; the protocol's own packets read off the
wire; `ip ospf cost` changing the metric; and the OSPF-vs-static administrative-distance rule.


