**The partners' return routes to 192.168.10.0/24 are STATIC and stay static.**  The return path
is not what this case tests; keeping it static means the forward path is the only variable, so a
reply that comes back proves the DUT had a FORWARD route for a prefix it can only have learned
from OSPF.  (Static AD 1 also beats OSPF AD 110 on the partners, so an OSPF-learned copy of the
same prefix cannot silently change the return path mid-case.)

NOTE on the shape of the test: both usable host NICs land on the DUT, so there is no scapy host
behind a partner.  The far side of every routed flow is therefore the PARTNER ITSELF answering
from its own loopback -- which is exactly what is wanted here, because that loopback is the
prefix under test.

