STEP 5 -- DEPTH: `passive-interface`, both halves of the guide's claim measured ON THE WIRE.

  Guide authority, OSPF FOG C613-22055-00 REV E p.14 ("Passive-interfaces"), verbatim:
  > "An interface can be configured in OSPF as a passive-interface: passive-interface vlan50
  >  This means that the network associated with this interface can be advertised into OSPF
  >  using the network command, but no OSPF protocol packets will be sent out of this
  >  interface."
  Two separate claims -- "still advertised" AND "no protocol packets" -- and the host NIC on
  vlan10 lets both be measured directly, which is rare.

  5a. BEFORE.  OSPF packets on eth3: one Hello every ~11 s (step 2f: 21:41:29.185 and
      21:41:40.188).  swi_b's view of the DUT's vlan10 network (21:49:53):
        x960-28YSQ# show ip route database
        O       192.168.10.0/24 [110/2] via 10.0.61.1, vlan61, 00:10:35
        S    *> 192.168.10.0/24 [1/0] via 10.0.61.1, vlan61 weight 1
      (swi_b's own static wins the FIB at AD 1 -- the underlay's return route -- but the OSPF
       copy is plainly in its RIB, which is what "advertised" means here.)

  5b. APPLY (21:50:00-21:50:03):
      awplus(config)# router ospf 1
      awplus(config-router)# passive-interface vlan10

  5c. CLAIM 1 -- "no OSPF protocol packets will be sent out of this interface":
      $ sudo timeout 30 tcpdump -i eth3 -n -c 3 'proto ospf'
      **0 packets captured** over a full 30 s (0 received by filter, 0 dropped by kernel),
      against an expected ~3 Hellos at the measured 10 s timer.  PROVEN.
      Corroborated independently by the counted leg below: L5-passive-eth3.pcap is the ONLY
      capture in this case that contains **no `IP p89 ... -> 224.0.0.5` frame at all** -- every
      other leg caught one or two in passing.  Two instruments, same answer.

  5d. CLAIM 2 -- "the network ... can still be advertised into OSPF".  Read from the NEIGHBOUR,
      so it is the protocol's view and not the DUT's own (21:50:50-21:50:53):
        x960-28YSQ# show ip route database
        O       192.168.10.0/24 [110/2] via 10.0.61.1, vlan61, 00:00:41    <- still there
        x960-28YSQ# show ip ospf database router 1.1.1.1
          LS age: 45
           Number of Links: 4
            Link connected to: a Transit Network  (Link ID) DR address: 10.0.61.2
            Link connected to: a Transit Network  (Link ID) DR address: 10.0.41.2
            Link connected to: Stub Network       (Link ID) Network/subnet number: **192.168.10.0**
            Link connected to: Stub Network       (Link ID) Network/subnet number: 192.168.48.0
      The DUT's Router LSA, as swi_b holds it, STILL carries 192.168.10.0 as a stub link.
      PROVEN.  (Note the LSA was re-originated -- LS age 45 s -- when the interface went
      passive: it changed from a transit link to a stub, which is exactly right, because the
