8. TEARDOWN AND RESTORE
-----------------------
OSPF is removed from ALL THREE devices before the next case, because RIP (T43856) is the next
case and OSPF's AD of 110 beats RIP's 120 -- leaving OSPF up would silently keep the RIP route
out of the FIB and make T43856 unmeasurable (STANDING-ORDERS §1, "one case's leftovers never
become another case's variable").

  swi_d (pointer restored FIRST, before deleting the file):
    awplus(config)# boot config-file flash:/default.cfg
    awplus(config)# no router ospf 1
    awplus(config)# no service ospf
    % Save the config and restart for this change to take effect.
    awplus# delete flash:/ckT43857.cfg   -> Delete flash:/ckT43857.cfg? (y/n)[n]:y
                                         -> Successful operation          (tools/ckyn.py)
  swi_b, swi_a:
    (config)# no router ospf 1
    (config)# no service ospf
    % Save the config and restart for this change to take effect.

  INSTRUMENT NOTE (the known cfg.py trap, reproduced): the `% Save the config and restart...`
  advisory is BENIGN, but `cfg.py` stops at the first `% ` and abandoned the rest of each
  batch -- here only the trailing `end`.  **cfg.py's cleanup still returned all three consoles
  to the exec prompt**, verified with `peek.py` immediately afterwards (`awplus#`,
  `x960-28YSQ#`, `awplus#`), so no console was left in config mode.  Worth knowing: the abort
  path is safe, it is the UNSENT REMAINDER that costs you.

  VERIFIED AFTER TEARDOWN (21:59:30-22:00:31):
    swi_d# show boot    -> Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg (file exists)
    swi_d# dir flash:   -> no ckT43857.cfg remains
    swi_d# show ip route -> the FOUR CONNECTED routes only, as at group C start
    swi_b# show ip route -> C 10.0.61.0/30, C 10.70.70.1/32, S 192.168.10.0/24, S 192.168.48.0/24
    swi_a# show ip route -> C 10.0.41.0/30, C 10.90.90.1/32, S 192.168.10.0/24, S 192.168.48.0/24
    swi_b# / swi_a# show running-config | include ospf -> EMPTY on both

  TEARDOWN DIFF (STANDING-ORDERS §1), on ALL THREE devices this case touched, against the
  pre-case captures taken 21:33 NZDT (`python3.8 tools/rcdiff.py <pre> <post>`; rcdiff PRINTS
  differences but EXITS 0, so the OUTPUT is read, never the exit code):
    swi_b : NO OUTPUT -- byte-identical to pre-case.
    swi_a : NO OUTPUT -- byte-identical to pre-case.
    swi_d : ONE added line --
        @@ -31,2 +31,4 @@
         loop-protection loop-detect ldf-interval 1 fast-block
        +!
        +no service ospf
         !

  🔴 DECLARED RESIDUE / FINDING F-5 -- `no service ospf` STICKS IN running-config ON A DEVICE
  THAT **BOOTED** WITH `service ospf`, AND ONLY ON THAT DEVICE.

  Measured, all three devices, same commands, same minute:

    | device | how ospfd was started          | `show process \| include ospf` after teardown | running-config |
    | ------ | ------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- | -------------- |
    | swi_d  | **at BOOT**, from the staged   | `1701  ospfd ... sleep`  (still running)       | **`no service ospf`** |
    |        | flash:/ckT43857.cfg (step 7)   |                                                |                |
    | swi_b  | at RUNTIME, by `service ospf`  | `32355 ospfd ... sleep`  (still running)       | nothing        |
    | swi_a  | at RUNTIME, by `service ospf`  | (same)                                         | nothing        |

  So it is **not** "the daemon is still running" -- ospfd is still running on all three, which is
  the documented `no service X` behaviour (memory `awplus-service-gated-routing-daemons`) and is
  what the `% Save the config and restart` advisory announces.  The distinguishing factor is
  that swi_d **booted from a config containing `service ospf`**; the negation is then rendered
  as an explicit running-config line, and no further configuration removes it.

  **Why it matters beyond this case:** every reboot-persistence limb on a service-gated routing
  protocol (OSPF, RIP, BGP, PIM, VRRP, BFD ...) will leave a `no service <proto>` line behind,
  and the case's teardown diff will NOT come back clean however carefully the case is written.
  Any future tester who sees it should recognise it rather than chase it.  It is cosmetic -- the
  line is semantically identical to the platform default (the service is off, no routing process
  exists, `show ip ospf` reads "OSPF Routing Process not enabled", and the route table is back
  to the four connected routes) -- but it IS a running-config difference and it is declared here
  rather than glossed over.

  **Disposition:** carried into T43856, whose own reboot limb reloads the DUT and will show
  whether the line clears on the next boot.  Resolved or escalated at group C hand-back; the
  DUT's boot pointer, flash and route table are already back to the pre-case state, so nothing
  about the bench is left in an unknown condition.  For platforms/x960.md.


9. WHAT THIS CASE DID NOT PROVE
--------------------------------
- **Multi-area OSPF.**  Everything here is a single backbone area 0.0.0.0.  Inter-area (IA),
  ABR/ASBR behaviour, stub/NSSA and route summarisation were NOT exercised.
- **`redistribute` into OSPF** (connected / static / RIP / BGP) and `default-information
  originate`, both documented in the guide, were NOT exercised.
- **OSPF authentication** (`ip ospf authentication [message-digest]`) was NOT exercised.  The
  wire capture shows `Authentication Type: none (0)`, which is the default.
- **Route filtering / distribute-list / route-maps** in OSPF were NOT exercised.
- **Cost changing the PATH.**  `ip ospf cost` was proven to change the metric, but this bench
  gives each loopback exactly one way in, so cost could not be shown to MOVE traffic.
  A third routed leg, or a prefix advertised by both partners, would be needed.
- **OSPF equal-cost multipath.**  Same reason.  (Static ECMP is T43861, already PASS.)
- **swi_b's OSPF-64 route limit** was nowhere near approached (2 networks advertised).


