RUN END: 2026-10-15 22:02 NZDT (2026-10-15 09:02 UTC)

VERDICT: PASS -- OSPFv2 on the x960-56YSQ is gated behind `service ospf` (and the gate is
real: `router ospf` is rejected ~1 s after it and accepted within 7 s), forms Full adjacencies
with two neighbours over two interfaces in 17 s, installs routes LEARNED FROM THE PROTOCOL
(code `O`, AD 110, each carried by the owning partner's Router LSA in the LSDB) for prefixes
reachable no other way -- no static route exists at any point -- and FORWARDS over them 5/5 and
5/5 on the wire while a control flow to an unrouted prefix is dropped and the instrument
control answers in every single leg.  Withdrawal was proven ON THE WIRE in the strongest
available form: one advertisement removed on the neighbour, with the port, the link and the
adjacency all still up and the second neighbour's prefix still at 5/5 in the SAME leg, the
subject fell 5/5 -> 0/5 in under 7 s and swi_b re-originated its Router LSA with one fewer link;
re-advertising restored 5/5.  The guide's claims were tested, not assumed: AD 110 loses to a
static at AD 1 **in the dataplane**, attributed by 5 ICMP time-exceeded sourced from the static
next hop; `ip ospf cost 50` moves the metric 2 -> 51; `passive-interface` stops OSPF packets
dead (0 in 30 s, against one Hello per 10 s) while the DUT's Router LSA held at the NEIGHBOUR
still advertises the network, and user traffic is untouched.  `no router ospf 1` returns the
dataplane to the step-1 baseline, and the whole feature survives a real 61 s reboot.
One finding recorded (F-5, `no service ospf` sticks in running-config on a device that booted
with `service ospf`) -- a teardown/display artefact, not a failure of OSPFv2, declared in full
in section 8 and carried forward.
